<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asturias</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a> is an autonomous community and historic region on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, separated from the Meseta by the Cantabrian Mountains. It takes its name from the Astures, the last Iberian peoples conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After the Muslim conquest of 711 it became the core of the Christian Kingdom of Asturias, and since 1388 the heir to the Castilian, and later Spanish, throne has borne the title Prince of Asturias.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asturias.jpg" alt="Asturias" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Banderas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Asturias.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of <b>Asturias</b> arose in the Cantabrian Mountains of northern Spain a few years after the Umayyad conquest of the Visigothic Kingdom, which began in 711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Castile began as a frontier district of the kingdom of <b>Asturias</b> and León on the upper Ebro and Duero, named for the castles that guarded it against Muslim raids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of León grew out of the Kingdom of <b>Asturias</b>, the small Christian realm founded in the mountains of northern Spain after the Muslim conquest of 711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their fusion with the Hispano-Romans produced a nobility that thought of itself as one people, the gens Gothorum or Hispani; its remnants fled to <b>Asturias</b> and Septimania, and in Asturias, Joseph F. O'Callaghan writes, joined the native leaders in a new aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The soprano Barbara Hendricks sang it when she received the Prince of <b>Asturias</b> Award for the Arts in 2002, and John Legend sang it at the Hope for Haiti Now telethon in 2010.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Olmedo in Castile, the forces of Henry IV of Castile met those of his half-brother Alfonso, Prince of <b>Asturias</b>, in their succession war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
